2. **Duration of Exposure**: The rate of a photochemical reaction is also directly proportional to the duration of exposure to light. This is because the rate of the reaction is directly proportional to the amount of light energy absorbed by the photosensitizer. As the duration of exposure to light increases, the photosensitizer absorbs more light energy, leading to a higher rate of the photochemical reaction.

In summary, the rate of a photochemical reaction involving a photosensitizer is directly proportional to both the concentration of the photosensitizer and the duration of exposure to light. This relationship can be expressed as follows:

\[
\text{Rate of photochemical reaction} \propto \text{Concentration of photosensitizer} \times \text{Duration of exposure}
\]

