","To find the normal line to the surface \(x^{2}+y^{2}+9 z^{2}=56\) at the point \((4,2,-2)\), we first compute the gradient of the function \(F(x,y,z) = x^{2}+y^{2}+9 z^{2}-56\). The gradient is given by the partial derivatives:
\[
\nabla F = \left( \frac{\partial F}{\partial x}, \frac{\partial F}{\partial y}, \frac{\partial F}{\partial z} \right) = (2x, 2y, 18z).
\]
At the point \((4,2,-2)\), the gradient is:
\[
\nabla F(4,2,-2) = (2 \cdot 4, 2 \cdot 2, 18 \times (-2)) = (8, 4, -36).
\]
The normal vector to the surface at \((4,2,-2)\) is \(\mathbf{n} = (8, 4, -36)\). The normal line to the surface at \((4,2,-2)\) is the line with direction vector \(\mathbf{n}\) and passing through \((4,2,-2)\). The equation of the normal line is:
\[
\boxed{(x-4) = 8 t, \ (y-2) = 4 t, \ (z+2) = -36 t}.
\]",relevance,"# Task Description
