Write an equation of the line tangent to the graph of a differentiable function \( f \) at the point \( (a, f(a)) \).
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
The equation of the line tangent to the graph of a differentiable function \( f \) at the point \( (a, f(a)) \) is given by the equation of a tangent line at a point on a curve. The equation is given by:

\[
y = f'(a)(x - a) + f(a)
\]

Here, \( f'(a) \) is the derivative of \( f \) evaluated at \( x = a \), which represents the slope of the tangent line at the point \( (a, f(a)) \).

Thus, the equation of the tangent line is:
- The slope of the tangent line is \( f'(a) \).
- The tangent line passes through the point \( (a, f(a)) \).

Using the point-point form of a line, the equation is:
\[
y - f(a) = f'(a)(x - a)
\]
Rearranging the equation, we get:
\[
y = f'(a)(x - a) + f(a)
\]

This equation represents the line tangent to the graph of \( f \) at the point \( (a, f(a)) \).
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

