","To find the number of roots of the equation \(\sin x = \frac{x}{20}\), we analyze the graphs of the two functions \(y = \sin x\) and \(y = \frac{x}{20}\).

The first function is \(y = \sin x\), which is an periodic function with period \(2\pi \approx 6.285\). The range of \(\sin x\) is \((-1, 1)\).

The second function is \(y = \frac{x}{20}\), which is a straight line with slope \(\frac{1}{20}\).

The graph of \(y = \sin x\) oscillates between \(-1\) and \(1\), while the graph of \(y = \frac{x}{20}\) is a straight line starting at \((0, 0)\).

At \(x = 0\), both functions have the same value, \(0\), so \(x = 0\) is a root.

The maximum value of \(\sin x\) is \(1\), and the minimum is \(-1\). Thus, the equation \(\sin x = \frac{x}{20}\) has roots when \(-1 \leq \frac{x}{20} \leq 1\), which implies \(-20 \leq x \leq 20\).

The slope of the line \(y = \frac{x}{20}\) is \(\frac{1}{20}\), which is small. The graph of \(\sin x\) oscillates between \(-1\) and \(1\).

