<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
The decay length (\(\lambda_d\)) of an evanescent wave is given by:  
\[
\lambda_d = \frac{\lambda}{2\pi (n_{\text{eff}} - n_f)}
\]  
where:  
- \(\lambda\) is the wavelength of the light  
- \(n_{\text{eff}}\) is the effective refractive index of the material  
- \(n_f\) is the refractive index of the surrounding medium (e.g., air or air)  

Given:  
- \(\lambda = 1 \mu m = 1 \times 10^{-6} \text{ m}\)  
- \(n_{\text{eff}} - n_f \approx 0.3\)  

Assuming \(n_f = 1\) (air), the decay length is:  
\[
\lambda_d = \frac{1 \times 10^{-6} \text{ m}}{2\pi (0.3)} \approx 5.3 \times 10^{-7} \text{ m} = 530 \text{ nm}
\]  

Thus, the decay length of the evanescent wave is \(\boxed{530 \text{ nm}}\).
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

