What is the maximum percentage change in the wavelength of a 2.00 Å photon scattered by an electron?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","The maximum percentage change in the wavelength of a 2.00 Å photon scattered by an electron is given by the Compton shift formula:  
\[
\Delta \lambda = \frac{h \lambda}{m_e c^2} \left(1 - \frac{\theta}{2\pi}\right)
\]  
where:  
- \(\Delta \lambda\) is the shift in the wavelength,  
- \(\lambda\) is the incident wavelength,  
- \(\theta\) is the scattering angle,  
- \(\hbar = h /m_e\) is the reduced Planck constant,  
- \(m_e\) is the electron mass,  
- \(c\) is the speed of light.  

The maximum shift occurs when \(\theta = 0^\circ\) (head-on scattering), so:  
\[
\Delta \lambda_{\text{max}} = \frac{h \lambda}{m_e c^2}
\]  
Substituting the values:  
- \(\lambda = 2.00 \times 10^{-10} \text{ m}\) (2.00 Å)  
- \(\lambda_0 = 2.998 \times 10^8 \text{ m/s}\) (speed of light)  
- \(h = 6.626 \times 10^{-34} \text{ J s}\) (Planck's constant)  
- \(m_e = 9.11 \times 10^{-31} \text{ kg}\) (electron mass)  

\[
\Delta \lambda_{\text{max}} = \frac{6.626 \times 10^{-34} \times 2.00 \times 10^{-10}}{9.11 \times 10^{-31} \times (2.998 \times 10^8)^2} \approx 1.1 \times 10^{-10} \text{ m} = 1.1 \times 10^{-4} \text{ nm}
\]  

The percentage change in wavelength is:  
\[
\frac{\Delta \lambda}{\lambda} \times 100\% = \frac{1.1 \times 10^{-10}}{2.00 \times 10^{-10}} \times 100\% = 0.55\%
\]  

Thus, the maximum percentage change in the wavelength of a 2.00 Å photon scattered by an electron is \(\boxed{0.55\%}\).",relevance,"# Task Description
