To estimate the tensile yield strength \(\sigma_{yr}\) due to the presence of hard particles, we use the equation for the stress of the dislocation-particle interaction:

\[
\sigma_{yr} = \frac{2\pi G b^2}{d}
\]

where:
- \(\sigma_{yr}\) is the tensile yield strength due to the presence of the particles.
- \(G\) is the shear modulus of the alloy.
- \(b\) is the Burgers vector.
- \(d\) is the effective diameter of the particles.

Given:
- \(d = 10^{-8}\) m (particle diameter)
- \(G = 26 \times 10^9\) N m\(^{-2}\) (shear modulus)
- \(b = 0.286 \times 10^{-9}\) m (Burgman vector)

Substitute the values into the formula:

\[
\sigma_{yr} = \frac{2\pi \times 26 \times 10^9 \times (0.286 \times 10^{-9})^2}{10^{-8}}
\]

\[
\sigma_{yr} = \frac{2\pi \times 26 \times 10^9 \times 8.21 \times 10^{-19}}{10^{-8}}
\]

\[
\sigma_{yr} = \frac{5.3 \times 10^{-2}}{10^{-8}}
\]

\[
\sigma_{yr} = 5.3 \times 10^6 \text{ N m}^{-2}
\]

\[
\sigma_{yr} = 5.3 \times 10^6 \text{ Pa} = 5.3 \text{ MPa}
\]

Thus, the estimated tensile yield strength due to the presence of these particles is \(\boxed{5.3 \text{ MPa}}\).
