\[
Q^2 = (P_f - P_i) \cdot (P_f - P_i)
\]

Substituting the components of \( P_f \) and \( P_i \):

\[
Q^2 = \left( \frac{E_f}{c}, \mathbf{p}_f \right) - \left( \frac{E_i}{c}, \mathbf{p}_i \right) \cdot \left( \frac{E_f}{c}, \mathbf{p}_f \right) - \left( \frac{E_i}{c}, \mathbf{p}_i \right)
\]

Expanding the dot product:

\[
Q^2 = \left( \frac{E_f}{c} - \frac{E_i}{c} \right)^2 + \left( \mathbf{p}_f - \mathbf{p}_i \right) \cdot \left( \mathbf{p}_f - \mathbf{pi}_i \right)
\]

Expanding the square and using the dot product:

\[
Q^2 = \left( \frac{E_f - E_i}{c} \right)^2 + \mathbf{p}_f \cdot \mathbf{p}_f - 2 \mathbf{p}_f \cdot \mathbf{p}_i + \mathbf{p}_i \cdot \mathbf{p}_i
\]

